---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Routes from "../../components/Routes.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const REQ = "server/src/iki_server/entities/requests.py";
const ACCESS = "server/src/iki_server/entities/access.py";
const MODELS = "server/src/iki_server/entities/models.py";

const H = {
  short: "One owner, one service, two writers",
  how: "Intake, sending, the two sides and the service's own work",
  rules: "What sending checks, and who writes what",
  ref: "Routes, code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "intake", label: "What a service takes" },
  { id: "sending", label: "Sending" },
  { id: "sides", label: "The two sides" },
  { id: "work-items", label: "Work items" },
  { id: "queue", label: "A service's queue" },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      The asking project owns a request: it's made there, by its Editors, as any record. Sending it
      picks one service for good, and links the request and the records its <code>sends</code> fields
      name into the service's project, through a <code>ProjectLink</code> with <code>via_request</code>. From
      then on each side writes only its own fields, and the database holds that. What the core
      does at the bench lives in work items in its own project, which nobody else can link or
      reference; only the deliverable goes back (<Adr n={[132, 211]} />). What each project can read
      through those links is on <PageLink to="Sharing across projects" />.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <h3 id="intake">What a service takes</h3>
    <p>
      A project's Admins choose the request types it takes, and from whom: every project in its
      registry, or the projects they name. A parent type takes its subtypes, so an intake for <code
        >iki/requests.Request</code
      > takes Plain, Research and QC requests alike. A project can't name itself, nor a project in
      another registry; each change is audited as <code>project.intake_changed</code> against the one
      before it, under a lock on the project (<Adr n={[132, 149]} />, <Path p={REQ} sym="def set_intake(" />, <Path p={REQ} sym="def accepted_by(" />).
    </p>
    <p>
      The services an asker is offered are the unarchived projects of its registry whose intake takes
      the type from it. Any other answers 404 with the same words whether it exists or not, so a
      project's name doesn't leak to those it doesn't serve (<Path p={REQ} sym="def services_for(" />, <Path p={REQ} sym="def not_a_service(" />).
    </p>

    <h3 id="sending">Sending</h3>
    <p>
      <code>POST /entities/&#123;ref&#125;/submit</code> locks the request and checks, in order: it's a
      request type's record; the caller is an Editor of the asking project; the revision is current
      (412 otherwise); it isn't purged, archived or of a frozen type; it hasn't been sent; the
      service's intake accepts it; the asking project allows sharing; and every record it sends is
      shareable into the service. Then it makes the <code>Request</code> row, links the request and
      each sent record into the service, audits <code>request.submitted</code> in the asking project
      and <code>request.received</code> in the service, and tells the service's coordinators (<Path
        p={REQ}
        sym="def submit("
      />, <Path p={MODELS} sym="class Request(" />).
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>sends</code> is a declaration on the type, which its subtypes inherit; neither side
      chooses more fields to expose at run time (<Adr n="0211" />). For a reference to a registry
      entry, the asking project's own record of it is what's sent (<Path p={REQ} sym="def sent_records(" />; entries
      and records are on <PageLink to="Identity and HRIDs" />). Records named later, when the
      asker edits a sent request, go to the service too (<Path p={REQ} sym="def send(" />). A link a
      request made can't be taken out while it's needed: the request stays with its service for good,
      and a record it still sends stays until the asker takes it off the request (<Path
        p={REQ}
        sym="def why_kept("
      />).
    </p>

    <h3 id="sides">The two sides</h3>
    <p>
      The type's <code>service_fields</code> are the service's; every other field is the asker's. A
      person writes as the asking side as an Editor of the asking project, and as the service side as
      an Editor of the service; someone in both writes both. Each side sets its state fields with the
      lab roles it holds in its own project (<Path p={REQ} sym="def sides(" />).
    </p>
    <Excerpt file={REQ} start="^ASKER, SERVICE = " />
    <p>
      Every write path checks the fields against the sides (<Path p={REQ} sym="def refused_fields(" />). The
      database checks it again: a trigger on each request type's tables reads which side the write
      is from the session setting <code>iki.request_side</code>, which the server sets inside a
      savepoint, and refuses a change to the other side's columns (<Path
        p="server/src/iki_server/entities/migrations/0015_requests.py"
        sym="iki_request_guard()"
      />, <Path p={REQ} sym="def writing(" />, <Path p={REQ} sym="def install_guards(" />).
    </p>

    <h3 id="work-items">Work items</h3>
    <p>
      A type with <code>work_item: &#123; of: request &#125;</code> is the service's private record of
      work on a request. It can be made only in the project the request was sent to, and no other
      project can link it, reference it or reuse it, including through someone who belongs to both
      (<Adr n={[132, 211]} />, <Path p={REQ} sym="def check_work_item(" />, <Path
        p={ACCESS}
        sym="def why_held("
      />). <code>iki/requests</code> ships one with a schedule, an instrument, internal notes and its
      own Queued, Scheduled, Running, Done and Cancelled states:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file="plugins/requests/types/work_item.yaml" start="^work_item:" />

    <h3 id="queue">A service's queue</h3>
    <p>
      Sent requests are linked into the service, so they're in its grid and search. Search has a
      built-in <code>sent_to</code> column on the request's service, which gives a service its queue as
      an ordinary search, and finds unsent requests too (<Path
        p="server/src/iki_server/entities/search.py"
        sym='"sent_to": Column('
      />). Each side lists the other by name alone, through <code>sent-from</code> and <code
        >sent-to</code
      >, so it can filter by them without seeing anything else of theirs.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Which side writes what" open>
        <p>
          Before sending, a service field is refused with "is the service's: the project the request
          is sent to sets it, once it's submitted". After, a field of the other side names the project
          that changes it. A list's table belongs to one side as a whole. An unsent request is the
          asking project's alone, so the trigger lets every write through until
          the <code>Request</code> row exists (<Path p={REQ} sym="def refused_fields(" />, <Path
            p={REQ}
            sym="def guarded_tables("
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="A request goes to one service, for good">
        <p>
          A request sent once is refused a second time, naming the service it went to. It can't be
          recalled, only withdrawn through its type's states: in <code>iki/requests</code>, the asking
          project's coordinators set <code>standing</code> to Withdrawn, with a reason (<Path
            p="plugins/requests/types/request.yaml"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Sharing out needs both sides' consent">
        <p>
          A project that doesn't allow sharing can't send requests, and a sent record must be one the
          asking project may share into the service. What a request brings into a project goes no
          further: it's read and referenced there, and not linked into a third project (<Adr
            n={[67, 132]}
          />, <Path p={ACCESS} sym="def shareable_into(" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the ADRs and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0132" /> describes the database guard as refusing an update to the other side's
            columns. In code it's broader: it also refuses the service's inserts and deletes on a sent
            request's tables, and any write to the other side's list tables.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <Routes tags={["requests"]} />
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={REQ} />: intake, sending, the two sides and work items</li>
        <li><Path p={MODELS} sym="class Intake(" />, <Path p={MODELS} sym="class IntakeSource(" />: what a service takes</li>
        <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/requests.py" />: the request routes</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0067" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0120" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0132" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0211" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
